  ],
  "slug": "blob-coalescent-a28c",
  "summary": "Paired genomic and transcriptomic data now allow variant-level NMD prediction, while splicing metrics require explicit correction for NMD-targeted transcripts.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is supervised or model-based inference of RNA-level regulatory outcomes from transcriptomic data, here focused on nonsense-mediated decay (NMD). TrunCat, a gradient-boosting classifier, predicts NMD sensitivity of protein-truncating variants from TOPMed paired whole-genome and RNA-seq data, reaching about 78% ROC-AUC. A second preprint corrects percent-spliced-in estimates for NMD-targeted transcripts using a single-parameter linear fractional model and Gaussian mixtures separating NMD-sensitive transcripts. BRAID addresses a parallel problem in splicing quantification by calibrating RNA-seq ΔPSI estimates with split conformal intervals derived from RT-PCR residuals, achieving 0.971 RT-PCR coverage across 139 cassette exons. What is new is the linkage of paired genomic and transcriptomic data to variant-level NMD prediction, and explicit parameterized correction of NMD-induced bias in splicing metrics, with uncertainty quantified against orthogonal RT-PCR.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-07"
}
